%%%%%%BOARD 1
https://tinyurl.com/2b9jvqnl
qx|A1|st||rh||ah|Board 1 IMPs   -   On December 18, 2024|pn|hondo717,aspiring,vs_bbo,Rustgi65|
md|3SA87H5DAK98643C97,S965HJT92DTCAKT42,SKT3HK8764DQJ7CQ8,SQJ42HAQ3D52CJ653|sv|0|
mb|P|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In 3rd seat, you tend to be very aggressive about preempts. Marty Bergen has what he calls the
^*BRule of 24^*n for 3rd seat. According to that rule after two passes, the number of HCP in the first two hands averages around 16 or so. That means 3rd and 4th seat average around 24 HCP.|
PG| ***** 1 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Therefore, in 3rd seat, if you take your HCP and subtract from 24, you can get a rough idea of how strong 4th seat is. In theory in this situation West would average around 13 HCP or so and I would want to preempt to make it harder for West to bid.|
PG| ***** 2 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The rule is quite off here but it usually works well. In any case, I am going to open 3@D. I would open 1@D in 1st and 2nd seat. But here since partner passed and they likely have a heart or a spade fit preempting has a lot going for it.|
PG| ***** 3 ***** |


mb|3D|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 4 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|CA|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I have 9 sure tricks and a possible 10th. I will make 4 if the @HA is onside or I can set up a long heart. Whenever you have a 5-card suit opposite a singleton in a trump contract one possibility is to set up that suit if it breaks 4-3, which is over 60% assuming you have the entries to ruff 3 times.|
PG| ***** 5 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Or sometimes, if the suit is stronger (e.g. you have the A and K) you only need to ruff twice. It is worthwhile to keep that in mind the next time you encounter that situation.|
PG| ***** 6 ***** |


pc|C8|pc|C3|pc|C7|
pg| ***** 7 ***** |


