qx|A6|st||rh||ah|Board 6 IMPs   -   On December 18, 2024|pn|hondo717,vs_bbo,Rustgi65,aspiring|
md|4ST62HAJ852DAKT7C6,SQ98HK93DJ9842CA4,SK4HQT64D5CQJ9832,SAJ753H7DQ63CKT75|sv|e|
mb|P|mb|1H|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|North has a clear 3@C |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||AT|showing 10-12 and 4 hearts. North has 8 HCP, 2 length points, 3 for singleton and 1 for doubleton. Technically using Bergen evaluation, North is actually good enough to make a 4@D |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=splinters,Splinter^*N|CP||FP||at|. But I think 3@C is better because North only has 8 HCP and lacks aces and kings, making slam very unlikely.|
PG| ***** 91 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is one of the many advantages of |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||AT| when you have a marginal game force with 4 card support, you can bid 3@C to show a limit raise and raise to 4 even if partner does not accept the invitation.|
PG| ***** 92 ***** |


mb|3C|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|My hand illustrates another good bidding principle to follow. If partner makes a limit raise and opener has a singleton they should always bid 4 even with a weak opener. As it turns out I would bid 4 anyway. But if I were weaker, I would also bid 4 with my  singleton.|
PG| ***** 93 ***** |


mb|4H|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 94 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|D4|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is the lead I would choose also. If no obvious lead, leading low from a long weak suit tends to be the least likely to cost a trick. BTW, this is an interesting hand to play.|
